var _markdown = "\n\nSeeders populate your database with initial or fixture data. They are the bridge between an empty schema and a usable environment: local development, preview deployments, and CI all start from the same seeding contract. Seeders are **ordered by filename**, **idempotent by contract** (safe to re-run), and **factory-powered**, so they share the model-aware data generation used by tests.\n\n## Defining a Seeder [#defining-a-seeder]\n\n```ts title=\"src/database/seeders/01-users.ts\"\n// src/database/seeders/01-users.ts\nimport { defineSeeder } from '@kwiva/data'\n\nexport default defineSeeder('users', async ({ factory, db }) => {\n  await factory(User).count(10).create()\n  await factory(User).create({ email: 'admin@acme.dev', role: 'admin' })\n})\n```\n\n| Shape   | Detail                                                |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Name    | First argument, used by `--seeder` and status output  |\n| Context | `{ factory, db }` — factories and the active database |\n| Body    | Plain async code; run each seeder in filename order   |\n\n## Running Seeders [#running-seeders]\n\n```bash title=\"terminal\"\nkwiva db:seed                # run all seeders in order\nkwiva db:seed --seeder=users # run only the named seeder\nkwiva db:seed --fresh        # drop + migrate + seed\nkwiva db:reset               # drop + migrate + seed (equivalent fresh path)\n```\n\n| Flag             | Effect                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--seeder=users` | Run a single seeder, named by its first argument        |\n| `--fresh`        | Drop all tables, re-migrate, then seed                  |\n| `db:reset`       | The full reset command — sees seeders as the final step |\n\n> \\[!WARNING]\n> `db:reset` and `--fresh` are destructive: they drop every table. They belong in development, preview, and test environments — never against production data.\n\n## Ordering by Filename [#ordering-by-filename]\n\nSeeders run in **lexicographic filename order**, so `01-users.ts` runs before `02-posts.ts`. Prefix numerically when a seeder depends on records created by an earlier one:\n\n```plaintext title=\"ordering-by-filename.txt\"\nsrc/database/seeders/\n  01-users.ts        # tenants, users, roles\n  02-posts.ts        # authored content\n  03-tags.ts         # many-to-many links after both sides exist\n```\n\nThe name argument and the filename work together: the filename sets the order, the name is the handle you pass to `--seeder`.\n\n## The Idempotency Contract [#the-idempotency-contract]\n\nSeeders are **safe to re-run**. The contract is anchored in upsert-based defaults: records with a stable identity (a unique email, slug, or code) are created when absent and updated when present, rather than appended on every run. This makes `db:seed` deterministic across runs — the tenth run leaves the same database as the first.\n\nConcretely, prefer stable identifiers when seeding:\n\n```ts title=\"the-idempotency-contract.ts\"\nexport default defineSeeder('users', async ({ factory }) => {\n  await factory(User).create({ email: 'admin@acme.dev', role: 'admin' })\n  await factory(User).create({ email: 'editor@acme.dev', role: 'editor' })\n})\n```\n\nRe-running inserts these once, not twice. For ephemeral volume use factories with random data (see [Factories](/docs/data/factories)) — they are intended to vary per run.\n\n## Seeding Relations [#seeding-relations]\n\nBecause factories understand relations, seeders compose naturally:\n\n```ts title=\"seeding-relations.ts\"\nexport default defineSeeder('posts', async ({ factory }) => {\n  const admin = await User.first({ where: { email: 'admin@acme.dev' } })\n  await factory(Post).count(5).create({ author: admin })\n})\n```\n\nRelation arguments on a factory create resolve to real related rows, and many-to-many links are seeded the same way — create both sides, then attach. Prefer resolving related records by stable lookup (email, slug, code) over positional assumptions; filename ordering guarantees the lookup target exists.\n\n## Lookups Over Creation [#lookups-over-creation]\n\nThe idempotency contract rewards **lookups** over blind creation. When a seeder needs a record that may already exist from an earlier run or an upstream seeder, resolve it first:\n\n```ts title=\"lookups-over-creation.ts\"\nexport default defineSeeder('settings', async ({ db }) => {\n  const tenant = await Tenant.first({ where: { slug: 'acme' } })\n  const settings = await Settings.first({ where: { tenantId: tenant.id } })\n\n  if (settings) await settings.update({ plan: 'pro' })\n  // otherwise create; either way the end state is identical to a fresh run\n})\n```\n\nThis is the shape that makes `db:seed` deterministic across runs and across environments — the end state is a function of the seed list, not of how many times it has run.\n\n## Environment-Aware Seeding [#environment-aware-seeding]\n\nSeeder code is just code, so it can read configuration and branch on environment when that is genuinely useful:\n\n| Pattern                                      | Use case                                                                                   |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Small canonical records in every environment | `db:seed` on fresh databases so apps boot with tenants, roles, admin accounts              |\n| Larger fake volume in development            | Factories with random data make preview UIs feel populated                                 |\n| Production-first gating                      | Never seed throwaway volume into production data; seed only canonical records, if anything |\n\nKeep this split explicit in the seeder body and reserve heavy fake data for development and preview runs. After a fresh clone, `kwiva db:reset` is the one local command that puts a working, seeded database in front of you.\n\n> \\[!TIP]\n> Do not put credentials or secrets in seeders — `admin@acme.dev` style records with obviously fake, per-environment passwords belong in local dotenv, not in seed code that may be shared.\n\n## When Seeding Happens [#when-seeding-happens]\n\n| Context                     | How                                                                                |\n| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Fresh local dev             | `kwiva db:reset` after `create-project`                                            |\n| Preview deploys             | `kwiva db:seed --fresh` in the deploy pipeline                                     |\n| CI                          | Explicit `db:seed` against the disposable test database                            |\n| Tests — **not recommended** | Tests build their own fixtures via factories; seeders are coarse-grained by design |\n\n## What's Next [#whats-next]\n\n1. [Factories](/docs/data/factories) — the generation engine inside every seeder\n2. [Migrations](/docs/data/migrations) — what runs before seeders\n3. [Testing](/docs/testing) — why tests use factories rather than seeders\n4. [CLI: Database Commands](/docs/cli/database-commands) — seed flags and output\n";
